;
; bridge_line.pio -- the USB bridge's transmitter on the ESC signal wire:
; 19200 baud 8N1 in seven PIO instructions (docs/design/usb-bridge.md,
; "The wire").
;
; The pad is the state machine's OUT and SET pin, and its side-set pin, which
; writes the pad's direction. One bit is eight state machine cycles, as in
; pio_uart_rx.pio, whose program receives on the same pad in a second state
; machine of the same block at the same clock divider. That receiver hears
; every byte this program sends: the line's own echo.
;
; A FIFO word holds a byte in bits 0 to 7, sent least significant bit first,
; and in bit 8 what the pad does after the byte's stop bit: 1 keeps it driven
; high, for a byte with more of its frame behind it; 0 lets it go, for the
; last byte of a frame. Between frames the machine stalls on `pull` with the
; pad an input, held high by its pull-up. Within a frame, if core1 is late
; with the next byte, it stalls there with the pad driven high.
;
; Start bit: driven low, 8 cycles. Data bits: 8 cycles each. Stop bit:
; driven high, 8 cycles; then `out pindirs` takes bit 8 and `pull` the next
; byte, a cycle each, so the bytes of a frame are 82 cycles, 10.25 bit
; times, apart, and the last byte's pad is let go 80 cycles after its start
; bit began.
;
; test/pio_bridge_emu.py assembles this file and pio_uart_rx.pio and runs
; both state machines on one pad, one cycle at a time.

.program bridge_tx
.side_set 1 opt pindirs

.wrap_target
    pull                        ; stall for a byte; the pad as the last byte left it
    set pins, 0      side 1 [6] ; start bit: driven low
    set x, 7                    ; eight data bits
bitloop:
    out pins, 1                 ; one data bit, least significant first
    jmp x-- bitloop         [6] ; eight cycles a bit
    set pins, 1             [7] ; stop bit: driven high, 8 cycles
    out pindirs, 1              ; bit 8: 1 keeps the pad driven, 0 lets it go
.wrap
